From 2b3c4d5e6f7a8b9c0d1e2f3a4b5c6d7e8f9a0b1c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: DockSeed <dockseed@proton.me>
Date: Tue, 21 Jul 2026 16:30:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH v2] arm64: dts: a733: add cache geometry so cacheinfo uses the DT
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Give every cpu@N its L1 geometry and every L2/L3 node a size, a line size,
a set count and `cache-unified`. Patch `arm64-dts-a733-cpu-topology` described the cache topology
but left the geometry out, and without it the kernel ignores the DT
description entirely.

v1 -> v2: added `cache-sets` / `i-cache-sets` / `d-cache-sets`. v1 left
them out because the associativity was unsourced; it has since been found
in the Arm TRMs (below), so the sets are now computed, not guessed.

WHAT THE BOOT TEST SHOWED, before this patch (2026-07-21, our chain, 8 GiB
board RS504-D8):

  [    0.049479] cacheinfo: Unable to detect cache hierarchy for CPU 0
  cpu0 index2: level=2 type=Unified shared_cpu_list=0-7
  cpu6 index2: level=2 type=Unified shared_cpu_list=0-7
  size= ways_of_associativity= coherency_line_size= number_of_sets=

WHAT IT SHOWS WITH v1 OF THIS PATCH (same board, same session):

  dmesg | grep -c cacheinfo  ->  0        (the warning is gone)
  cpu0 index2: level=2 type=Unified size=128K  shared_cpu_list=0
  cpu6 index2: level=2 type=Unified size=256K  shared_cpu_list=6
  cpu0 index3: level=3 type=Unified size=1024K shared_cpu_list=0-7

So the private L2 that `arm64-dts-a733-cpu-topology` describes is now what the kernel actually
sees, and the DSU L3 is correctly shared by all eight cores.

WHY IT FAILED WITHOUT THE GEOMETRY, from the source in this tree:

  * arch/arm64/kernel/cacheinfo.c: `ci_leaf_init()` sets only `level` and
    `type`. arm64 never fills size/ways/sets/line-size from CCSIDR_EL1, so
    geometry can only come from the DT. `arm64-dts-a733-cpu-topology`'s comment claiming the kernel
    reads them from CCSIDR_EL1 was wrong.
  * drivers/base/cacheinfo.c: when `cache_setup_of_node()` fails,
    `cache_setup_properties()` sets `use_arch_info = true` (arm64 defines
    `use_arch_cache_info()` as `true`). `cache_leaves_are_shared()` then
    returns `level != 1` for every pair, i.e. every cache above L1 is
    declared shared system-wide. It is sticky: later calls are gated on
    `!use_arch_info`, so one early failure decides the whole boot.

SIZES -- A733 UM v0.92 Figure 1-1, the SoC-level
statement of what Allwinner configured:

  Cortex-A55 x6   L1 I 32 KB   L1 D 32 KB   L2 128 KB
  Cortex-A76 x2   L1 I 64 KB   L1 D 64 KB   L2 "265 KB"  [sic]
  DSU             L3 1 MB

ASSOCIATIVITY -- Arm Technical Reference Manuals for the cores this SoC
integrates. These are fixed by the core design, not by the integrator:

  Cortex-A55 TRM (ARM 100442, r1p0) sec. A6.1:
    "4-way set associative L1 instruction cache", "4-way set associative
    L1 data cache", 64-byte lines.
  Cortex-A55 TRM sec. A7.1:
    "An optional 4-way, set-associative L2 cache with a configurable size
    of 64KB, 128KB or 256KB."
  Cortex-A76 TRM (ARM 100798, r4p1) sec. A2.1.1 / A2.1.6:
    "A 64KB, 4-way, set associative L1 instruction cache" and the same
    for the L1 data cache -- not configurable on this core.
  Cortex-A76 TRM sec. A7.1:
    "An 8-way set associative L2 cache with a configurable size of 128KB,
    256KB or 512KB."
  DynamIQ Shared Unit TRM (ARM 100453, r4p1) sec. 3.1:
    "The 256KB, 512KB, 1MB, 2MB, and 4MB caches are 16-way set
    associative. All caches have a 64-byte line length."

sets = size / (ways * line size), giving:

  A55 L1 I/D  32 KB  / (4 * 64) = 128
  A55 L2     128 KB  / (4 * 64) = 512
  A76 L1 I/D  64 KB  / (4 * 64) = 256
  A76 L2     256 KB  / (8 * 64) = 512
  DSU L3       1 MB  / (16 * 64) = 1024

Note the DSU associativity is size-dependent -- the same TRM paragraph
makes the 1.5 MB and 3 MB configurations 12-way. 16-way is correct for
the 1 MB this SoC carries, but it must not be copied blindly to another
DSU part.

THE A76 L2 "265 KB" IS A DOCUMENT ERROR. The Cortex-A76 offers 128, 256
or 512 KB only (TRM sec. A7.1), so 265 KB cannot exist; it is a
transposed digit. Entered here as 256 KB.

WHAT IS STILL NOT PROVEN: which of the legal sizes the A733 actually
implements for the A76 L2. The only source is the figure that contains
the typo, and reading it back from sysfs would be circular -- that value
comes from this very DT. The silicon answer is CCSIDR_EL1 with CSSELR_EL1
selecting level 2 on an A76 core, which needs EL1 code. Until that register
is read on an A76 core, 256 KB rests on the TRM's list of legal sizes; a
different reading means this value must be corrected.

Assisted-by: Claude
